1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,898
You have lost your keys. You walk out from the front door, scanning the path, but no

2
00:00:04,898 --> 00:00:07,346
luck, and now you have drifted a long way.

3
00:00:07,346 --> 00:00:10,320
Keep going, or go back to the door and start again?

4
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:16,246
Going back sounds like giving up. But in many searches, restarting makes the search faster

5
00:00:16,246 --> 00:00:21,422
on average. Today we will see why, and meet a new result about the best possible way

6
00:00:21,422 --> 00:00:22,100
to restart.

7
00:00:23,067 --> 00:00:26,149
Let's make the search simple enough for maths.

8
00:00:26,149 --> 00:00:29,298
Here is a searcher on a line, starting at zero.

9
00:00:29,298 --> 00:00:34,323
Every instant it takes a tiny random step, left or right, like a coin flip.

10
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:37,434
Somewhere along the line is the target.

11
00:00:37,434 --> 00:00:40,868
The search ends the moment the walker touches it.

12
00:00:41,267 --> 00:00:48,040
To see a whole search at once, turn the line upright, so position goes up, and let time

13
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:51,465
run across. Each search draws a jagged path.

14
00:00:51,465 --> 00:00:54,035
This one hits the target quickly.

15
00:00:54,433 --> 00:00:59,493
This one is unlucky. Its first steps go the wrong way, and it wanders off.

16
00:00:59,493 --> 00:01:03,937
It will come back eventually, but that can take a very long time.

17
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:08,848
Run thousands of searches and record how long each one takes.

18
00:01:08,848 --> 00:01:13,981
Most are short. But a few are enormously long, and they never quite stop coming.

19
00:01:14,367 --> 00:01:20,118
For a walker on a line, those rare long searches are so long that the average search

20
00:01:20,118 --> 00:01:23,748
time is infinite. This is a classic fact, long known.

21
00:01:23,748 --> 00:01:28,815
The walker always finds the target, but on average you would wait forever.

22
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:33,050
Now add one rule. Every so often, a timer rings.

23
00:01:33,050 --> 00:01:38,805
If the walker has not found the target yet, it jumps straight back to zero and starts

24
00:01:38,805 --> 00:01:40,768
a fresh, independent attempt.

25
00:01:41,167 --> 00:01:47,369
Watch the unlucky walker again. The timer rings, and the long excursion is cut short.

26
00:01:47,369 --> 00:01:51,967
On a later attempt it heads the right way and finds the target.

27
00:01:52,333 --> 00:01:55,177
Restarting throws away hopeless attempts.

28
00:01:55,177 --> 00:02:01,349
With a sensible timer, the average search time drops from infinite to an ordinary, finite

29
00:02:01,349 --> 00:02:04,885
number. That is the surprising power of restarting.

30
00:02:05,867 --> 00:02:11,299
How should the timer work? The simplest choice is a fixed timer: it rings after exactly

31
00:02:11,299 --> 00:02:13,235
the same time on every attempt.

32
00:02:13,633 --> 00:02:19,208
Another choice is a random timer. In every moment it has the same small chance of ringing,

33
00:02:19,208 --> 00:02:22,614
like a radioactive atom that could decay at any moment.

34
00:02:22,614 --> 00:02:25,153
Sometimes it rings early, sometimes late.

35
00:02:25,533 --> 00:02:32,169
In general, a restart recipe is any rule for picking the wait before each restart, picked

36
00:02:32,169 --> 00:02:35,226
fresh and independently on every attempt.

37
00:02:35,226 --> 00:02:38,133
Fixed, random, or any blend of the two.

38
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:44,467
For any recipe, two numbers matter: how long one attempt lasts on average, and the chance

39
00:02:44,467 --> 00:02:49,749
that it succeeds. If attempts last two seconds and one in four succeeds, you need four

40
00:02:49,749 --> 00:02:51,960
attempts, so eight seconds in total.

41
00:02:52,333 --> 00:02:58,789
So the average search time is the average attempt length, divided by the success chance.

42
00:02:59,767 --> 00:03:02,770
In real life you do not know where the keys are.

43
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:05,711
You have a hunch: probably close, possibly far.

44
00:03:05,711 --> 00:03:08,527
Here is our map of where the target might be.

45
00:03:08,527 --> 00:03:11,719
Near is likely, and the chance fades with distance.

46
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:16,935
Average over this map, and try random timers with different rates.

47
00:03:16,935 --> 00:03:20,153
Restart too often, and the walker never gets far.

48
00:03:20,153 --> 00:03:25,998
Too rarely, and it wanders off. The sweet spot is a rate of one, giving an average search

49
00:03:25,998 --> 00:03:27,312
time of exactly one.

50
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:31,401
But that only compares random timers with each other.

51
00:03:31,401 --> 00:03:35,172
Could a fixed timer, or some strange blend, do better?

52
00:03:35,172 --> 00:03:38,244
Out of every possible recipe, which is best?

53
00:03:38,244 --> 00:03:42,364
Pause, and think: how could you ever check infinitely many?

54
00:03:43,333 --> 00:03:47,280
In 2025, Evans and Ray turned the question around.

55
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:53,437
Start with a recipe, and ask: for which map is this recipe the natural choice?

56
00:03:54,233 --> 00:04:00,585
Take our random timer with rate one. Its success curve shows the chance that a single

57
00:04:00,585 --> 00:04:03,574
attempt finds a target at each distance.

58
00:04:03,574 --> 00:04:07,385
Close by, it is almost certain. Far away, it fades.

59
00:04:07,767 --> 00:04:13,975
Their prescription: square the success curve, then rescale it so the total chance is

60
00:04:13,975 --> 00:04:17,523
one. For this timer, that gives exactly our map.

61
00:04:17,523 --> 00:04:19,815
It is called the conjugate map.

62
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:26,275
They showed that on its conjugate map, a recipe is balanced: tiny tweaks do not help.

63
00:04:26,275 --> 00:04:31,849
But balanced is not the same as best. A flat spot could be a small dip, with a deeper

64
00:04:31,849 --> 00:04:32,964
valley elsewhere.

65
00:04:33,333 --> 00:04:38,365
Their checks against other recipes were numerical, and stayed inside two families of

66
00:04:38,365 --> 00:04:40,941
timers. So the big question was still open.

67
00:04:41,933 --> 00:04:45,353
The new result settles it for the walker on a line.

68
00:04:45,353 --> 00:04:51,185
Take any restart recipe whose conjugate map makes sense, and whose average time on that

69
00:04:51,185 --> 00:04:56,951
map is finite. Then it beats every other recipe on that map, and it is the only recipe

70
00:04:56,951 --> 00:04:58,493
that achieves the best.

71
00:04:58,867 --> 00:05:04,626
Every other recipe means any positive, finite waits, picked independently each attempt.

72
00:05:04,626 --> 00:05:10,384
Smart restarts that react to the walker, and restarts that cost extra time, are outside

73
00:05:10,384 --> 00:05:11,179
the theorem.

74
00:05:12,167 --> 00:05:17,770
Here is the key idea. The average length of an attempt can be built from the success

75
00:05:17,770 --> 00:05:21,705
curve, using a weight for every pair of distances, x and y.

76
00:05:21,705 --> 00:05:24,640
The weight is simply the smaller of the two.

77
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:27,575
Swap x and y, and the weight stays the same.

78
00:05:28,467 --> 00:05:35,007
Now bring in a rival recipe, and compare its success curve with ours as a ratio, distance

79
00:05:35,007 --> 00:05:39,636
by distance. The rival's average time becomes a sum over pairs.

80
00:05:39,636 --> 00:05:45,808
The pair x, y carries u over v, and the swapped pair carries v over u, with the same

81
00:05:45,808 --> 00:05:46,323
weight.

82
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:53,353
Add the two together. u over v plus v over u always equals two, plus u minus v squared,

83
00:05:53,353 --> 00:05:54,500
over u times v.

84
00:05:54,900 --> 00:06:00,075
Try u equals two and v equals one. Two plus a half is two and a half.

85
00:06:00,075 --> 00:06:04,500
And two, plus one squared over two, is also two and a half.

86
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:08,425
The twos add up to exactly the cost of our recipe.

87
00:06:08,425 --> 00:06:14,771
Everything left over is a square divided by positive numbers, so it can never be negative.

88
00:06:14,771 --> 00:06:16,252
No rival can beat it.

89
00:06:17,133 --> 00:06:22,766
When is the leftover zero? Only when every square is zero, so the rival's success curve

90
00:06:22,766 --> 00:06:28,205
matches ours everywhere. And a success curve is like a fingerprint: it pins down the

91
00:06:28,205 --> 00:06:31,053
timer exactly. So the best recipe is unique.

92
00:06:31,933 --> 00:06:37,703
Credit where it is due. This pairing trick is known, and a closely matching version appears

93
00:06:37,703 --> 00:06:43,093
in work by Davila and Topp. What is new is using it here to prove the global best and

94
00:06:43,093 --> 00:06:47,341
its uniqueness, even against rivals whose average time is infinite.

95
00:06:48,333 --> 00:06:54,794
Back to our map. The theorem says the random timer with rate one is the unique best recipe,

96
00:06:54,794 --> 00:06:56,853
with average search time one.

97
00:06:57,233 --> 00:07:02,981
A computer check agrees. Random timers with rates one quarter and nine quarters both

98
00:07:02,981 --> 00:07:07,908
give four thirds. A blend of two timers gives about one point two seven.

99
00:07:07,908 --> 00:07:13,793
Thirty six random blends land between one point zero zero two and one point four five.

100
00:07:14,167 --> 00:07:20,087
And every fixed timer is infinitely bad on this map, because a distant target needs an

101
00:07:20,087 --> 00:07:26,215
absurdly lucky attempt. That last point is our own quick calculation, not from the paper.

102
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:31,377
No finite list of checks can cover infinitely many recipes.

103
00:07:31,377 --> 00:07:33,320
That is the proof's job.

104
00:07:34,300 --> 00:07:39,443
The paper also studies a different search: a walker hopping between a few rooms.

105
00:07:39,443 --> 00:07:43,686
Each room has an exit, and the search ends when the walker leaves.

106
00:07:43,686 --> 00:07:46,900
A restart sends it back to the room it started in.

107
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:50,282
Here the map says which room the walker starts in.

108
00:07:50,282 --> 00:07:55,292
The pairing trick now needs the network to be reversible: as much traffic flows from

109
00:07:55,292 --> 00:08:00,362
one room to another as flows back. Then the squared success prescription always gives

110
00:08:00,362 --> 00:08:01,316
the best recipe.

111
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:06,783
The new part is the converse. Under a technical condition, if the prescription works

112
00:08:06,783 --> 00:08:10,051
for every recipe, then the network must be reversible.

113
00:08:10,051 --> 00:08:12,956
So the prescription is a test for reversibility.

114
00:08:13,833 --> 00:08:18,957
Here is a network that fails: three rooms in a one way loop, with exit rates one, two

115
00:08:18,957 --> 00:08:22,815
and three. Apply the prescription to a random timer of rate one.

116
00:08:22,815 --> 00:08:27,939
The average time is two thousand three hundred and thirty two over four thousand four

117
00:08:27,939 --> 00:08:29,385
hundred and fifty three.

118
00:08:29,767 --> 00:08:35,043
Now slow the timer to nine tenths. The average drops, by about one ten-thousandth.

119
00:08:35,043 --> 00:08:40,448
Tiny, but exact. The prescribed recipe is not the best, so without reversibility the

120
00:08:40,448 --> 00:08:41,671
prescription fails.

121
00:08:42,667 --> 00:08:48,142
How sure can we be? The main theorem and the converse have careful written proofs, but

122
00:08:48,142 --> 00:08:51,325
they are not formalised in the Lean proof checker.

123
00:08:51,325 --> 00:08:56,419
Only the finite pairing algebra and the three room numbers were checked in Lean.

124
00:08:56,800 --> 00:09:02,247
The paper has not been peer reviewed. The conjugate map and the pairing trick are earlier

125
00:09:02,247 --> 00:09:07,816
work, and whether these exact additions are new still needs a full check of the literature.

126
00:09:08,700 --> 00:09:14,676
So, three things. Restarting can turn an infinite average search into a finite one.

127
00:09:14,676 --> 00:09:20,148
Squaring a recipe's success curve gives a map where that recipe is balanced.

128
00:09:20,148 --> 00:09:26,700
And for a walker on a line, that recipe is the one best recipe, thanks to a hidden symmetry

129
00:09:26,700 --> 00:09:28,212
and a sum of squares.

130
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:33,430
What we still don't know: whether the same holds in two or three dimensions; what happens

131
00:09:33,430 --> 00:09:38,152
with smart or costly restarts; and the room networks that fail the technical condition.

132
00:09:39,033 --> 00:09:44,687
So next time you lose your keys, going back to the door might not be giving up.

133
00:09:44,687 --> 00:09:46,833
It might be the smartest move.
